🔷 2. TypeScript (TS)

TypeScript is a superset of JavaScript developed by Microsoft.
✅ What It Is

    Adds static typing and type checking to JavaScript.

    Transpiles down to plain JS (so browsers can run it).

    Helps prevent runtime bugs and makes large projects easier to maintain.

🧠 Key Characteristics
Feature	Description
Static typing	You declare types: let name: string = "Alice";
Interfaces and enums	Add structure to complex data.
Generics	Enable reusable, type-safe code.
Compile-time checking	Catches many errors before runtime.
JS compatibility	Any .js file is valid .ts code.
🧩 File Extension

    .ts

🔧 Common Uses

    Large-scale apps.

    Frameworks like Angular, React (with TSX), and Next.js.

⚛️ 3. JSX (JavaScript XML)

JSX is a syntax extension for JavaScript used mainly with React.
✅ What It Is

    Looks like HTML inside JS.

    Compiles (via Babel) to React.createElement() calls.

🧠 Example

function Hello() {
  return <h1>Hello, world!</h1>;
}

➡️ Compiles to:

function Hello() {
  return React.createElement("h1", null, "Hello, world!");
}

🧩 File Extension

    .jsx

🔧 Common Uses

    React components.

    Next.js and Gatsby front-end development.

🟦 4. TSX (TypeScript + JSX)

TSX = TypeScript + JSX combined.
✅ What It Is

    TypeScript files that use JSX syntax.

    Enables both static typing and React JSX rendering.

🧠 Example

interface Props {
  name: string;
}

const Hello: React.FC<Props> = ({ name }) => {
  return <h1>Hello, {name}</h1>;
};

🧩 File Extension

    .tsx

🔧 Common Uses

    React + TypeScript projects (Next.js, Vite, etc.)

🟧 5. EJS (Embedded JavaScript Templates)

EJS is a templating engine used mostly on the server side (Node.js).
✅ What It Is

    Lets you embed JS logic directly inside HTML.

    Used for rendering HTML pages dynamically on the backend.

🧠 Example

Template (index.ejs):

<h1>Hello, <%= user.name %>!</h1>
<ul>
  <% users.forEach(user => { %>
    <li><%= user.name %></li>
  <% }) %>
</ul>

Server code (Express.js):

app.set('view engine', 'ejs');
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.render('index', { users: [{name: 'Alice'}, {name: 'Bob'}] });
});

🧩 File Extension

    .ejs

🔧 Common Uses

    Server-side HTML rendering in Node.js apps.

    Often used with Express.js.

